  {"type": "problems", "h2": "Air conditioner faults we fix in older Oshawa homes",
   "lead": "A central air conditioner bolted onto 60-year-old ductwork fails in predictable ways. Here is what each symptom usually means and how we handle it.",
   "items": [
    {"problem": "The AC is 20 years old and runs on R-22 refrigerant", "solution": "R-22 is no longer produced, and the little that remains is costly. A small leak on an R-22 unit means a repair that may not be worth it. We test the system honestly, and if the charge is low we lay out the repair price beside a new cold-climate heat pump that cools and heats on a modern refrigerant."},
    {"problem": "The coil ices up every hot afternoon", "solution": "In a wartime house the supply ducts are often too small to move the air a modern coil needs, so the coil runs too cold and freezes. A plugged filter makes it worse. We thaw it, replace the filter, measure static pressure and tell you whether the real fix is a duct change or a smaller, properly matched unit."},
    {"problem": "The upstairs of a 1.5-storey house never cools", "solution": "Those half-storey bedrooms under the roof get one small supply duct and no return, so the AC cannot pull the hot air out. We balance what the ducts can do and, where that is not enough, add a single ductless head upstairs so the central system only has to cool the main floor."},
    {"problem": "The breaker trips when the compressor starts", "solution": "A compressor's start-up current is hard on a weak capacitor and an old breaker. We test the capacitor, contactor and amp draw first. If the breaker or the wiring from a 60-amp or early 100-amp panel is the weak point, we quote that as ESA-licensed electrical work rather than resetting it and hoping."},
    {"problem": "The condenser sits on a sinking pad against the brick", "solution": "A pad that has tilted over 20 years strains the line set and the refrigerant connections, and a unit jammed against a wall cannot pull air through its coil. We level the unit, check the line set for stress cracks and clear the airflow, and we recommend a better spot when the unit is replaced."},
    {"problem": "Water beside the furnace in the basement", "solution": "The condensate drain in an older basement often runs to a floor drain that has not been cleared in years, or to nowhere at all. We clear the line, fit a trap and, where there is no drain nearby, add a condensate pump so the water goes where it should."},
    {"problem": "Cold but clammy on humid lake days", "solution": "An oversized air conditioner cools the air fast and shuts off before it pulls the humidity out, which is why some Oshawa homes feel damp at 22 degrees. We check the sizing against the house's heat gain. A variable-speed heat pump at low speed fixes this better than any repair."},
    {"problem": "A north-end builder AC quit before its tenth summer", "solution": "Newer homes in Windfields and Kedron came with the AC the builder could get cheapest, and coil leaks around year ten are common. We test the coil before quoting and show you the repair price next to a heat pump, since the ducts in those homes can usually carry one without changes."}]},
