  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Replace the AC with a heat pump? What old ducts and Oshawa Power rates mean",
   "html": "<p>When an Oshawa air conditioner reaches the end, the replacement question is different from the one in a new subdivision. The ducts in a wartime or post-war house limit what you can bolt on, and the hydro rate from Oshawa Power decides whether a heat pump's winter heating is a bargain or just a nice extra.</p>"
           "<p>Our approach is to measure first. If the ducts can carry the airflow a 2 or 2.5 ton heat pump needs, a hybrid with your existing gas furnace is usually the best value: the heat pump cools in summer, heats on mild and moderate days, and the furnace takes over in deep cold. If the ducts cannot carry it, we say so and look at a ductless multi-zone system instead of forcing a unit that will freeze its coil every August.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Capacitor, contactor, fan motor or drain on a unit under 10 years: repair</li><li>Refrigerant leak on an R-22 unit of any age: replace</li><li>Compressor or coil on a unit past 12 years: compare both prices</li><li>Ducts that cannot carry the airflow: ductless, not a bigger central unit</li></ul>"
           "<p>The duct testing and chimney questions that come with a furnace swap are covered on our <a href=\"/oshawa/\">Oshawa HVAC page</a>. For symptom-by-symptom help with any air conditioner in Ontario, the province-wide <a href=\"/ac/repair-maintenance/\">AC repair and maintenance</a> page goes deeper.</p>"},
